window.AG_PRACTICE["jieqi"] = {"id":"jieqi","axis":"herb","seal":"草","scene":"art/scene-garden.jpg","title":"Seasonal Kitchen","titleZh":"節氣食療","sub":"Eating with the season — gently, and without overclaiming","subZh":"順時而食，溫和而不誇大","mentor":{"key":"yao","nm":"Yào Wēng","nmZh":"藥翁","line":"\"Food therapy will not cure anybody. What it does is stop you fighting the season. That is worth more than it sounds.\"","lineZh":"「食療治不好誰。它做的是讓你別跟節氣對著幹。這件事，比聽起來值錢。」"},"card":{"h":"Rhythm, Not Perfection 順時而非求全","hZh":"","p":"Spring supports the Liver, summer the Heart, late summer the Spleen, autumn the Lung, winter the Kidney. Seasonal eating is a small daily adjustment, not a cure — and it is not a substitute for medical care.","pZh":"春養肝、夏養心、長夏養脾、秋養肺、冬養腎。節氣飲食者，日常之微調，非治病之法，亦不能代醫療。"},"note":"Educational food-therapy ideas only. Individual constitutions differ, and anyone who is pregnant, taking medication, or managing a health condition should speak with a qualified practitioner before making changes. 本組為食療觀念之教學。體質各異；懷孕、服藥或有慢性疾病者，欲有所調整，宜先諮詢合格醫療人員。","items":[{"k":"match","q":"Match each season to the organ it is traditionally paired with.","qZh":"五季配五臟。","pairs":[{"l":"Spring 春","lZh":"","r":"Liver","rZh":"肝"},{"l":"Summer 夏","lZh":"","r":"Heart","rZh":"心"},{"l":"Late summer 長夏","lZh":"","r":"Spleen","rZh":"脾"},{"l":"Autumn 秋","lZh":"","r":"Lung","rZh":"肺"},{"l":"Winter 冬","lZh":"","r":"Kidney","rZh":"腎"}],"note":"Late summer is the damp stretch between summer and autumn — hot and heavy, the season the Spleen finds hardest. In the Pacific Northwest the damp arrives with the rain instead, which is a fair local translation of the same idea. 長夏者，夏秋之間濕重之時，脾所最苦。西北岸之濕多隨雨而至，其理相通。"},{"k":"mc","q":"Spring eating in TCM traditionally does what?","qZh":"春季飲食，傳統主張為何？","opts":[{"t":"Reduce sour, add a little sweet, and favour gently uplifting foods","c":"省酸增甘，宜疏達升發之品","ok":1,"note":"Sour astringes, and spring is the season of outward movement — so the classical advice is to ease off the sour and add mild sweetness to support the Spleen, which the rising Liver tends to press on. Young greens, sprouts, chives and mild aromatics fit the season. 酸主收，而春主升發，故省酸；增甘以扶脾，防肝木之乘。時蔬嫩芽、韭、辛香之輕者，皆合其時。"},{"t":"Eat heavily and warmly to store energy","c":"厚味溫補以蓄藏","ok":0,"note":"That is winter advice. Heavy warming food in spring tends to make the rising Liver qi more, not less, uncomfortable. 此冬令之說。春而厚溫，反助肝之升動。"},{"t":"Eat cold raw food to clear heat","c":"生冷以清熱","ok":0,"note":"Cold raw food in early spring burdens the Spleen while it is still recovering from winter. 早春生冷，反困脾陽。"},{"t":"Fast for several days","c":"數日辟穀","ok":0,"note":"Not a mainstream seasonal recommendation, and not appropriate general advice. 非節氣飲食之常法，亦非可通行之建議。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"What is San Fu Tian 三伏天, and what is the idea behind treating in it?","qZh":"三伏天為何？其治之理安在？","opts":[{"t":"The hottest stretch of summer — the idea is treating winter illness in summer 冬病夏治","c":"夏之最熱者。其理為冬病夏治","ok":1,"note":"Three ten-day periods calculated from the summer solstice. The reasoning: when yang qi in nature and in the body is at its peak, cold-natured chronic conditions that flare in winter are considered easiest to address. In practice this is done as 三伏貼 herbal patches or moxa by a practitioner. It is preventive and traditional, not a guaranteed outcome. 自夏至後推算，凡三候各十日。其理：天人陽氣俱盛之時，冬日易發之寒證最易調治。臨床多以三伏貼或艾灸行之，屬預防調養之法，非必效之保證。"},{"t":"The coldest ten days of winter","c":"冬之最寒十日","ok":0,"note":"That is 三九天, when the mirror practice 夏病冬治 is sometimes done. 此三九天，別有夏病冬治之法。"},{"t":"The three days after each solar term","c":"每節氣後三日","ok":0,"note":"San Fu is calculated from the solstice by the stem-branch calendar, not from each term. 三伏依干支自夏至推之，非每節氣之後。"},{"t":"A fasting period","c":"齋戒之期","ok":0,"note":"It is not a fast. 非齋期。"}]},{"k":"match","q":"Match each season to a food traditionally associated with it.","qZh":"時令配常用食材。","pairs":[{"l":"Summer 夏","lZh":"","r":"Mung bean, winter melon, lotus leaf","rZh":"綠豆、冬瓜、荷葉"},{"l":"Late summer 長夏","lZh":"","r":"Job's tears, adzuki bean, poria","rZh":"薏苡仁、赤小豆、茯苓"},{"l":"Autumn 秋","lZh":"","r":"Lily bulb, pear, white fungus","rZh":"百合、梨、銀耳"},{"l":"Winter 冬","lZh":"","r":"Walnut, black bean, ginger","rZh":"核桃、黑豆、生薑"},{"l":"Spring 春","lZh":"","r":"Young greens, chives, rose bud tea","rZh":"時蔬嫩芽、韭菜、玫瑰花茶"}],"note":"Read these as directions rather than prescriptions. Mung bean is cooling — welcome in July, unhelpful for someone who is already cold. That caveat applies to every item on this list. 此為方向，非為處方。綠豆性涼，暑月為宜，素體虛寒者則不宜。表中諸物，皆當如是讀。"},{"k":"mc","q":"What is the reasoning behind the saying \"radish in winter, ginger in summer\"?","qZh":"「冬吃蘿蔔夏吃薑」，其理為何？","opts":[{"t":"In winter the interior runs warm and can stagnate; in summer the interior runs cold from cold food and drink","c":"冬則陽氣內藏而易滯，夏則貪涼而中焦反寒","ok":1,"note":"The saying is counter-intuitive on purpose. Winter: we wrap up, yang gathers inside, food is rich — radish moves qi and helps digestion. Summer: we drink cold things all day and yang has gone to the surface, leaving the middle cool — ginger warms it. Both are corrections for what the season does to us, not matching the weather. 此語故作相反。冬則衣厚陽藏，食多厚味，蘿蔔下氣消食；夏則貪冷飲，陽浮於外而中焦反冷，薑以溫之。皆所以矯時令之偏，非順其表象。"},{"t":"Radish is warming and ginger is cooling","c":"蘿蔔性溫而薑性涼","ok":0,"note":"Reversed. Radish is cool and moves qi downward; ginger is acrid and warm. 顛倒矣。蘿蔔性涼而下氣，薑辛而溫。"},{"t":"They are simply what grows in each season","c":"不過各應其時而產","ok":0,"note":"Seasonality is part of it, but the saying is specifically about correcting the body's internal state. 應時而產亦其一端，然此語所指者，矯其內也。"},{"t":"It is a modern advertising slogan","c":"近世廣告之語","ok":0,"note":"It is a long-standing folk saying with a clear theoretical basis. 此俗諺流傳已久，且理有所本。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Autumn dryness affects which organ most, and what does the kitchen do about it?","qZh":"秋燥最傷何臟？食療如何應之？","opts":[{"t":"The Lung — moisten with white, soft, gently sweet foods","c":"肺 — 以白色柔潤甘淡之品潤之","ok":1,"note":"The Lung dislikes dryness, and autumn is dry. Pear, lily bulb, white fungus, almond and honey are the traditional autumn foods, all moistening rather than warming. The signs to watch for are a dry throat, a dry nose, dry skin and a cough with little phlegm. 肺惡燥而秋主燥。梨、百合、銀耳、杏仁、蜂蜜，皆潤而不溫。當察咽乾、鼻燥、膚乾、乾咳少痰。"},{"t":"The Kidney — with heavy warming meat","c":"腎 — 以厚味溫熱之肉","ok":0,"note":"That is winter. Heavy warming food in a dry autumn tends to make the dryness worse. 此冬令之法。秋燥而厚溫，反增其燥。"},{"t":"The Liver — with sour foods","c":"肝 — 以酸味","ok":0,"note":"Spring is the Liver's season. Sour does have a place in autumn for astringing, but the main autumn concern is dryness in the Lung. 春屬肝。秋亦有用酸以收者，然秋之要在肺燥。"},{"t":"The Spleen — with damp-draining beans","c":"脾 — 以利濕之豆","ok":0,"note":"Draining damp is late summer's task. Autumn wants moistening, which is close to the opposite. 利濕為長夏之事。秋當潤，適與之反。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"A person is always cold, tires easily and has loose stools. Which summer habit would you gently question?","qZh":"其人畏寒、易倦、便溏。夏日何習當婉言提醒？","opts":[{"t":"Iced drinks and cold raw food all day","c":"終日冰飲與生冷","ok":1,"note":"The picture suggests Spleen yang is already struggling; a steady stream of cold drink asks the middle burner to warm everything it receives. The suggestion is small and practical — room temperature instead of iced, cooked instead of raw at most meals — not a rule and not a cure. 其象已近脾陽不足，終日冷飲，中焦所耗益甚。所建議者小而可行：飲以常溫代冰，餐以熟食為主，如此而已，非戒律，亦非治法。"},{"t":"Sleeping with a window open","c":"開窗而眠","ok":0,"note":"Reasonable in summer; a draught directly on the neck and shoulders is the part worth mentioning. 夏夜開窗尚可；惟風直吹頸肩者宜避。"},{"t":"Walking in the morning","c":"晨起步行","ok":0,"note":"Gentle morning movement suits almost everyone. 晨間緩行，人多相宜。"},{"t":"Drinking warm tea","c":"飲溫茶","ok":0,"note":"Warm drinks are exactly what this constitution tends to prefer. 此等體質，本喜溫飲。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Which statement about food therapy is honest?","qZh":"論食療，何說為實？","opts":[{"t":"It supports and adjusts; it does not treat disease and does not replace medical care","c":"其用在調養，不治病，亦不能代醫療","ok":1,"note":"Food acts slowly and gently, which is its strength for daily rhythm and its limit for illness. Anything that is worsening, severe, or not resolving belongs with a clinician. Saying so plainly is what makes the rest of the advice trustworthy. 食性和緩，故宜於日常之調，而不足以治病。凡病加重、病重、久而不解者，當就醫。言之明白，其餘之言乃可信。"},{"t":"The right congee can resolve most chronic conditions","c":"得其粥則慢病多可癒","ok":0,"note":"Overclaiming, and it is the kind of claim that costs a practitioner credibility. 誇大之言，最損醫者之信。"},{"t":"Food therapy works the same for everyone","c":"食療人人相同","ok":0,"note":"Constitution is the whole point — cooling foods suit a hot constitution and burden a cold one. 因體而異，正其要義：涼品宜熱體而困寒體。"},{"t":"If a food is in a classical text it must be safe for everyone","c":"古籍所載必人人可食","ok":0,"note":"Classical texts also record contraindications, including for pregnancy. 古籍亦載禁忌，妊娠尤有專條。"}]},{"k":"mc","q":"Which of these is a genuine safety point when discussing common food-therapy ingredients?","qZh":"論常用食療之品，何者為真正之安全提醒？","opts":[{"t":"Some familiar ingredients interact with medication or are cautioned in pregnancy","c":"部分習見之品可與藥物相互作用，或妊娠當慎","ok":1,"note":"Familiar does not mean inert. Several common kitchen-herb ingredients carry pregnancy cautions or interact with blood-thinning and other medication. The right move is to name the caution and point to a qualified practitioner rather than to hand out a list. 習見不等於無力。若干日常藥食之品，或有妊娠之慎，或與抗凝等藥相涉。當明言其慎，並囑其諮詢合格醫者，而非逕付一單。"},{"t":"Cooking removes all cautions","c":"煮過則諸慎皆解","ok":0,"note":"Cooking changes some properties but does not remove interactions. 烹製可變其性，然不解相互作用之慮。"},{"t":"Small amounts are always fine","c":"少量必無妨","ok":0,"note":"Usually, but not universally — and \"usually\" is not the standard to give advice on. 多數如是而非盡然；「多數」不足為建議之準。"},{"t":"Only prescribed herbs need caution","c":"惟處方之藥當慎","ok":0,"note":"Kitchen ingredients used medicinally are used in medicinal amounts. 藥食同源者，用如藥則量亦如藥。"}]},{"k":"order","q":"Put the five seasonal focuses in the order of the year.","qZh":"依歲序排出五季所養。","seq":[{"t":"Spring · support the Liver","c":"春養肝"},{"t":"Summer · support the Heart","c":"夏養心"},{"t":"Late summer · support the Spleen","c":"長夏養脾"},{"t":"Autumn · support the Lung","c":"秋養肺"},{"t":"Winter · support the Kidney","c":"冬養腎"}],"note":"This is also a content calendar: five seasons, five organs, five sets of foods, five sets of signs to watch for. Seasonal care is not about perfection — it is about rhythm. 此亦一年之綱：五季、五臟、五類食材、五組當察之症。順時者非求其全，求其有節而已。"}]};
